=== file: README.md ===
# Invoice extraction

This workflow is deliberately fail-closed. It accepts invoice text for one manual run, asks the configured model to return typed fields, and sends flagged extractions to a person without including the invoice text in that handoff. The invoice text is not written to a file or explicitly retained by the workflow.

## Output adapter is a required deployment step

The plan specifies one agent, `InvoiceExtractor`, and an `OutputFile` file-writing tool. Giving that tool to the extractor would let the same agent read untrusted invoice text and write files. The standard file tool also does not guarantee the required atomic, unique-name, no-overwrite behavior. This project therefore does not grant `OutputFile` to any agent and halts after extraction rather than risk an unsafe write.

Before enabling output, provide a separately audited operator adapter that accepts only the typed extracted fields, writes only those fields as JSON, atomically chooses a unique filename, and never overwrites an existing file. Ensure the adapter can be approved by a person and cannot access the invoice text. The current single-agent plan does not define a separate writer agent or a Java adapter, so neither is invented here.

Configure the runtime so `gpt-6-luna` resolves only to the approved in-environment deployment. Do not configure an external fallback. The prompt masks PII, but masking is not a substitute for keeping model processing within the required environment.

The tests exercise extraction routing and fail-closed behavior. They do not claim that files are written, that filename collisions are handled, or that an operator adapter has been implemented.
=== end===

